        <p class="profile-detail-region">Life · Ancient Near East</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Darius the Great</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 550 BCE – 486 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 550 BCE; the eldest of five sons of Hystaspes, an Achaemenid noble and officer in Cyrus's army, and grandson of Arsames; his mother is variously given as Rhodogune or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irdabama">Irdabama</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">486 BCE, after thirty days of an unidentified illness, aged about sixty-four; his body was embalmed and entombed in October 486 BCE in the rock-cut tomb at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Lance-bearer (spearman) to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a>; King of Kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> from 522 BCE until his death in 486 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the killing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Gaumata</a> and the revolts of 522 BCE; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daric">daric</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dariuss-scythian-campaign">Scythian campaigns</a>; the Darius Canal; the punitive expedition against Eretria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> ending at Marathon; the rock-cut tomb at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a></span></div>
        </div>
