    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Darius became king in 522 BCE by killing the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Gaumata</a>, whom he called an impostor; his account of the events is generally regarded as a forgery that concealed the overthrow and murder of Cyrus's rightful successor Bardiya.</li><li>He put down revolts in most parts of the empire within a year, divided the empire into twenty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a> with fixed tributes, and introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daric">daric</a> as a universal currency.</li><li>His campaigns took the empire to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a> and into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, but the expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Eretria ended in defeat at Marathon in 490 BCE, and he died in 486 BCE while preparing a second.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>At Behistun, Darius had a trilingual inscription in Elamite, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> and Babylonian carved into a cliff-face to record his conquests; it later became a key source for the Old Persian language and for the decipherment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

