    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 550 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Darius is born, the eldest son of Hystaspes</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">522 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">With six nobles he kills <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Gaumata</a> at the fortress of Sikayauvati and takes the throne, then faces revolts in most parts of the empire</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">516 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, Aria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and winters in Gandhara before conquering the lands around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus River</a> in 515 BCE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">513 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a> on a bridge of boats and invades European Scythia, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> avoid battle</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">497 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He travels to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for the opening of the canal linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">490 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Persian army sent against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Eretria is defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon">Battle of Marathon</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">486 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Darius dies after a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> delays his planned expedition; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> succeeds him</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Darius was born around 550 BCE, the eldest of five sons of Hystaspes and a grandson of Arsames. His mother's identity is uncertain. According to the modern historian Alireza Shapour Shahbazi (1994), she was thought to have been a woman named Rhodogune; according to Lloyd Llewellyn-Jones (2013), recently uncovered texts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> indicate that she was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irdabama">Irdabama</a>, an affluent landowner descended from a family of local Elamite rulers, and Richard Stoneman likewise names Irdabama. Hystaspes was an officer in Cyrus's army and a noble of his court. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a> of Darius states that he was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> in 522 BCE and a second cousin of Cyrus in the paternal line, though this may have been a fabrication to legitimate Darius's accession.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> (III.139), Darius, “of no consequence at the time”, served as a spearman (doryphoros) in the Egyptian campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> (528–525 BCE); this is often interpreted to mean that he was the king's personal spear-carrier, an important role. Darius's own account likewise says that he served Cambyses as lance-bearer. A story told of Cyrus relates that, before crossing the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a>, he installed his son Cambyses as king and then dreamed that Darius had wings on his shoulders and stood upon the confines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Cyrus took this as a danger to the empire, wondered whether Darius was forming treasonable designs, and ordered Hystaspes back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> to watch his son strictly.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Darius the Great&#8221; cites where it covers Early life, Early revolts, Elimination of Intaphernes, Family:</p><ul><li>Pierre Briant, From Cyrus to Alexander: A History of the Persian Empire (Eisenbrauns, 2002)</li><li>Richard Stoneman, Xerxes: A Persian Life (Yale University Press, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii.jpg" alt="Cambyses II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stela_Cambyses_Apis_closeup.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jona Lendering, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sources give different accounts of how Darius came to the throne. The oldest records report that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> lost his mind, had his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Bardiya</a> murdered and died of an infected leg wound, after which Darius and six nobles went to Sikayauvati to kill a usurper, Gaumata, who had taken the throne by pretending to be Bardiya. In Darius's own account, written at Behistun, the murder of Bardiya was not known among the Iranians; a revolt against Cambyses broke out in his absence on 11 March 522 BCE, and on 1 July the people chose to be under Gaumata as “Bardiya”. No member of the Achaemenid family would rise against him for fear for their lives. Darius, praying for aid, killed Gaumata in September 522 BCE with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otanes">Otanes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intaphernes">Intaphrenes</a>, Gobryas, Hydarnes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megabyzus">Megabyzus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aspathines">Aspathines</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>'s account is dubious. The seven nobles debated the form of government, with Otanes urging a democratic republic, Megabyzus an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a> and Darius a monarchy, and then settled the matter by whose horse neighed first at sunrise. In Herodotus's telling, Darius's groom Oebares arranged for Darius's horse to neigh. Greek historians also say that Gaumata was a Magian placed on the throne by his brother Patizeithes after Bardiya's murder. The details of Darius's rise are generally acknowledged as forgery, used to conceal his overthrow and murder of Cyrus's rightful successor, Bardiya. To legitimise his rule he had a common origin with Cyrus fabricated by naming Achaemenes as founder of their dynasty; he was not from the house of Cyrus and his forebears, the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Darius the Great&#8221; cites where it covers Accession, Elimination of Intaphernes:</p><ul><li>Pierre Briant, From Cyrus to Alexander: A History of the Persian Empire (Eisenbrauns, 2002)</li><li>John Boardman, The Cambridge Ancient History (Cambridge University Press, 1988)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Early in his reign Darius reorganised the empire and the taxation he had inherited from Cyrus and Cambyses. He created twenty provinces called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a>, each under a satrap and each with a fixed tribute, paid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> measured by the Babylonian talent and in gold measured by the Euboic talent; the total from the satraps came to less than 15,000 silver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> was the only province that was not conquered land. Each satrap had a secretary, a treasurer and a garrison commander, and royal inspectors, the “eyes and ears” of Darius, checked on them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> was kept as the common language. Before 500 BCE he introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daric">daric</a>, a gold and silver coin that replaced barter and Lydian or Ionian coins and that was also recognised beyond the empire's borders. He improved the network of roads and way stations, built canals and a navy, and in 497 BCE travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to open the canal that joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>.</p><p>His campaigns reached the empire's territorial apex. In 516 BCE he marched through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, Aria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> to Gandhara, wintered there, and in 515 BCE conquered the lands around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus River</a>, appointing the Greek Scylax of Caryanda to explore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> from the mouth of the Indus to Suez. In 513 BCE he invaded European Scythia, and he then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and many cities of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. He built or expanded works at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and in Egypt, where he built and restored temples, among them those to Ptah and Nekhbet. These works employed workers and artisans of many nationalities, several of them deportees.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Darius the Great&#8221; cites where it covers Elimination of Intaphernes, Egyptian campaign, Invasion of the Indus Valley, Babylonian revolt:</p><ul><li>Pierre Briant, From Cyrus to Alexander: A History of the Persian Empire (Eisenbrauns, 2002)</li><li>Kaveh Farrokh, Shadows in the desert: ancient Persia at war (Osprey Publishing, 2007)</li><li>livius.org, Darius the Great: Death (Thames &amp; Hudson, 2017)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/skunkha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/skunkha.jpg" alt="Skunkha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/skunkha">Skunkha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Behistun.Inscript.Skunkha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">livius.org, Attribution</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By late 522 BCE there were revolts in most parts of the empire. They broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a>, Media, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Darius had a loyal army led by close confidants and nobles, and with their support suppressed all of them within a year. In his own words he killed nine “lying kings”, whom he depicted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a>. The Elamite leader Aschina was captured and executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>. The Babylonian revolt, led by Nebuchadnezzar III, held out against a large army for a year and a half, and the Scythian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skunkha">Skunkha</a> was captured and added to the rebels shown at Behistun. His reign also had killings among his own supporters. When the noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intaphernes">Intaphernes</a> mutilated two officers who barred him from the palace, Darius feared a revolt of the seven, had Intaphernes arrested with his son, relatives and friends able to bear arms, and, though no proof of a plan was found, killed his entire family except his wife's brother and her son. In Egypt he executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryandes">Aryandes</a> on a charge of treason.</p><p>Abroad, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> evaded his army in 513 BCE by feints, retreats and a scorched-earth strategy, and Darius ordered a halt after a month of losses to fatigue, privation and sickness. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a>, in which Eretria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> sent ships and troops and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, brought the Persian reoccupation of the Ionian and Greek islands, and a force Darius sent across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> was turned back by a storm and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a>. His army, 20,000 men under Datis and Artaphernes, took Eretria but was defeated at Marathon in 490 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam.jpg" alt="Naqsh-e Rostam" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naqsh-e_Rustam_necropolis_in_Iran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maasaak, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the defeat at Marathon, Darius began planning another expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a>, this time to command the imperial armies himself instead of leaving them to Datis. He spent three years preparing men and ships when a revolt broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The revolt worsened his failing health and prevented him from leading another army.</p><p>Darius died after thirty days of suffering from an unidentified illness, partly due to his part in crushing the revolt, at about sixty-four years old. In October 486 BCE his body was embalmed and entombed in the rock-cut tomb at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a>, which he had been preparing. Its inscription introduces him as “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-king">Great King</a>, King of Kings, King of countries containing all kinds of men, King in this great earth far and wide, son of Hystaspes, an Achaemenian, a Persian, son of a Persian, an Aryan, having Aryan lineage.” A relief beneath the tomb showing equestrian combat was carved later, under the Sasanian King of Kings Bahram II.</p><p>Darius had several wives, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atossa">Atossa</a> and Artystone, daughters of Cyrus, and Parmys, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Bardiya</a>. His eldest son, Artobazanes, was born to a daughter of Gobryas before Darius rose to power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a>, his eldest son by Atossa, became heir through her influence, since she had great authority in the kingdom and Darius loved her most of all his wives. Xerxes had contested the succession with Artobazanes and became king as Xerxes I, so that the empire was again ruled by a member of the house of Cyrus. Xerxes completed the construction projects still under way.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription.jpg" alt="Behistun Inscription" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%DA%A9%D8%AA%DB%8C%D8%A8%D9%87_%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%DB%8C%D9%88%D8%B4_(_%D8%A8%DB%8C%D8%B3%D8%AA%D9%88%D9%86(.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Korosh.091, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Darius ruled the empire at its territorial peak, when it included much of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>'s coastal regions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and Northeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. His changes in provincial administration, coinage, weights and measures, roads and language made the empire centralised and unified. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daric">daric</a> was a major boost to international trade, and the new tax system led to the registration of measured and taxed land and to state banking and banking firms, one of the most famous being Murashu Sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>.</p><p>He supported the faiths of the lands he ruled so long as their adherents were “submissive and peaceable”, sometimes with grants from his treasury; he funded the restoration of the Israelite temple originally decreed by Cyrus, wrote to Gadatas in support of Greek cults, supported Elamite priests and built a temple to the Egyptian god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. His own faith was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a>, whom he believed had appointed him; he held that each rebellion was the work of druj, the enemy of Asha, and wrote “Ahura Mazda is mine, I am Ahura Mazda's”. Scholars have no consensus on whether he and his predecessors were influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>.</p><p>His inscriptions are a main source for his reign: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a> later became a key source for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> language and for the decipherment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, and Greek historiography, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, who gave him half of Book 3 and Books 4 to 6, records his campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Darius I (c. 550–486 BCE) became King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> in 522 BCE by killing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Gaumata</a>, an account historians generally regard as a cover for the overthrow of Bardiya. He suppressed revolts, organised the empire into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a>, issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daric">daric</a> and reached its greatest extent, but was defeated at Marathon.</em></p>

